## Final Judgement
## Summary of Key Findings

The evidence gathered clearly addresses both parts of the claim. First, regarding the "75,000 flu deaths last year in 2019" figure: the CDC estimated approximately 61,200 deaths for the 2018-2019 flu season, and 24,000-62,000 deaths for the 2019-2020 season. The 75,000 figure doesn't precisely match any CDC estimate, though it's closest to the 2017-2018 season's ~80,000 deaths. Second, and more critically, regarding "almost 0 in 2020": the CDC estimated 24,000-62,000 flu deaths for the 2019-2020 season, and no credible public health authority (CDC, WHO, or major medical institutions) reported U.S. flu deaths approaching zero. While the 2020-2021 season did see dramatically reduced flu activity due to COVID-19 mitigation measures, this was never characterized as "almost 0" deaths by any major health authority.

## Information Still Lacking

No further information is needed. The evidence is clear and sufficient to make a determination.

## Conclusion

The claim is refuted on multiple grounds. The CDC estimated tens of thousands of flu deaths for the 2019-2020 season (24,000-62,000), not "almost 0." Additionally, the "75,000" figure cited for 2019 doesn't match the CDC's official estimate of 61,200 for the 2018-2019 season. No major public health authority has reported U.S. flu deaths approaching zero in 2020. The claim significantly misrepresents both the baseline figure and the 2020 flu death numbers.

`refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is refuted on multiple counts. The CDC estimated approximately 61,200 flu deaths for the 2018–2019 season ([Time](https://time.com/5610878/2018-2019-flu-season/)), not 75,000, and the 2017–2018 season was actually the deadliest recent one at around 80,000 deaths ([CNN](https://www.cnn.com/2018/09/26/health/flu-deaths-2017--2018-cdc-bn/index.html)). More critically, flu deaths in 2020 did not approach zero; the CDC estimated 24,000–62,000 deaths for the 2019–2020 season ([Contagion Live](https://www.contagionlive.com/view/how-deadly-was-the-2019-2020-flu-season)), and while COVID-19 mitigation measures did reduce flu activity in 2020–2021, no credible public health authority ever characterized U.S. flu deaths as "almost 0."